Shapley value analysis (exact enumeration of all 32 coalitions; Figure~\ref{fig:shapley}) identifies naive cross-correlation as the sole positive contributor to FPR (Shapley${=}{+}0.192$) and permutation Granger as the strongest FPR suppressor (Shapley${=}{-}0.110$), the only method combining 0\% null-control FPR with high sensitivity.
Toda--Yamamoto's contribution to sensitivity is the weakest in the panel (Shapley${=}{-}0.033$ vs.\ ${-}0.242$ for the next weakest method); we retain it as insurance against unknown integration order, but note its severe power limitations at $T{\leq}10$.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/shapley_method_contribution.pdf}
\caption{Shapley value decomposition of method contributions to consensus FPR and FNR (exact enumeration of all $2^5{=}32$ coalitions, $K{=}\lceil 0.8|S|\rceil$ threshold). FPR is computed at $\alpha{=}0.0$ (3~seeds $\times$ 8~pairs${=}$24 observations); FNR at $\alpha{=}1.0$ (4~seeds, 6 reference pairs). Naive cross-correlation is the only positive FPR contributor (${+}0.192$); permutation Granger is the strongest FPR suppressor (${-}0.110$). Toda--Yamamoto contributes the least to sensitivity (${-}0.033$), consistent with its low power at $T{=}10$.}
\label{fig:shapley}
\end{figure}

Table~\ref{tab:fpr_fnr} reports the calibration results.
Table~\ref{tab:single-method-errors} complements this analysis by showing the practical consequences of relying on any single method: naive cross-correlation produces false positives at every $\alpha$ including the null control, Toda--Yamamoto misses true signals at $\alpha{=}0.75$, while the consensus at $K{\geq}3$ yields the correct verdict across all five contamination levels.

